\begin{abstract}
This white sheet presents a planetary-scale hydrodynamic control fabric based on the Niagara-Logic principle: continuous, controlled spillway behavior generalized into engineered multi-tier basin systems. The architecture integrates permanent spillways, reversible pump networks, variable-height labyrinth weirs, and autonomous intelligence looping. Real-time telemetry is fused with shallow-water solvers and surrogate CFD models to generate constraint-optimized actuation vectors. The system transforms catastrophic hydrodynamic surges into continuous, regulated flow regimes. A validation pathway consisting of formal stability proofs, historical storm replay simulations, and pilot basin deployments is proposed. The Hydrodynamic Control Fabric offers a structural path to eliminating a substantial fraction of catastrophic flood events worldwide.
\end{abstract}

\section{Introduction}

\subsection{Hydrodynamic Catastrophe as a Systems Failure}
Flooding is not merely a meteorological event; it is a systems failure arising from trapped hydraulic potential, insufficient spillway logic, and reactive basin management. Traditional infrastructure operates near static thresholds, guaranteeing overtopping and structural failure under nonlinear storm forcing.

\subsection{Niagara-Logic as a Generalized Engineering Principle}
Niagara Falls demonstrates a natural continuous spillway: excess hydraulic head from an upper basin is permanently bled into a lower basin through a stable, predictable flow regime. This work generalizes that principle into engineered multi-tier basin architectures capable of autonomous, continuous regulation.

\section{Hydrodynamic Control Fabric Overview}

\subsection{Core Concept}
The Hydrodynamic Control Fabric is a cyber-physical system that:
\begin{itemize}
    \item continuously ingests real-time hydrological telemetry,
    \item executes accelerated shallow-water and CFD surrogate solvers,
    \item computes constraint-optimized actuation vectors,
    \item modulates flow through pumps, weirs, and spillways,
    \item verifies downstream behavior and self-calibrates.
\end{itemize}

\subsection{Closed-Loop Intelligence Sequence}
\begin{enumerate}
    \item \textbf{Telemetry Ingestion:} IoT river gauges, soil moisture probes, hydrostatic pressure sensors, and meteorological streams.
    \item \textbf{Encrypted Transport:} ML-KEM-1024 and AES-256-GCM secure all telemetry.
    \item \textbf{State Estimation:} FPGA/GPU edge compute executes shallow-water PDEs and surrogate CFD models.
    \item \textbf{Constraint Optimization:} Structural stress limits, ecological flow baselines, and retention thresholds.
    \item \textbf{Actuation:} Reversible pumps, variable-height labyrinth weirs, permanent spillways.
    \item \textbf{Closed-Loop Verification:} Downstream flow meters compare actual vs. predicted displacement.
\end{enumerate}

\section{Niagara-Logic Multi-Tier Basin Architecture}

\subsection{Upper Basin: Retention and Pre-Flow}
\begin{itemize}
    \item Primary retention volume.
    \item Pump-assisted gravity routing.
    \item Continuous pre-flow spillways to prevent threshold accumulation.
\end{itemize}

\subsection{Mid-Tier Escarpment: Fine-Grained Control}
\begin{itemize}
    \item Labyrinth weirs for micro-adjustment of flow.
    \item Structural stress manifolds.
    \item Reversible pump stations for routing excess.
\end{itemize}

\subsection{Lower Basin: Discharge and Dissipation}
\begin{itemize}
    \item Flow regulation gates.
    \item Energy dissipation channels.
    \item Integration with downstream rivers or ocean outfalls.
\end{itemize}

\section{Hydrodynamic Physics and Control Theory}

\subsection{Shallow-Water Dynamics}
Let $h(x,t)$ denote water depth and $u(x,t)$ horizontal velocity. The shallow-water equations are:
\[
\frac{\partial h}{\partial t} + \nabla \cdot (h u) = 0,
\]
\[
\frac{\partial (h u)}{\partial t} + \nabla \cdot \left( h u \otimes u + \frac{1}{2} g h^2 I \right) = -g h \nabla z + S,
\]
where $z$ is bed elevation and $S$ represents pump/weir actuation.

\subsection{Closed-Loop Stability}
Define state vector $x(t)$ and control input $u(t)$. A Lyapunov function $V(x)$ must satisfy:
\[
V(x) > 0, \qquad \frac{dV}{dt} \leq 0,
\]
ensuring asymptotic stability under storm forcing.

\subsection{Overshoot Bounds}
Actuation latency $\tau_{\max}$ must satisfy:
\[
h(t) \leq h_{\text{safe}},
\]
preventing catastrophic overtopping.

\subsection{Safety Invariants}
Under telemetry failure:
\begin{itemize}
    \item spillways default to conservative gravity-bleed,
    \item pumps enter low-power continuous routing,
    \item weirs lock into safe positions.
\end{itemize}

